The second output of the true-bit X-step on atomPair at a sign sequence and at depth n equals the second component of that atom intersected with XPseq at n.

The invariant gives matching emptiness and the empty-or-neighbourhood alternative. For one inclusion, the output lies in the second component by the subset law, and it lies in XPseq because the padding of the restricted sign sequence agrees with the original sequence below n, so the atoms agree and the corresponding summand of the union contains the output. For the other inclusion, an element of the intersection belongs to some summand. If that padded prefix agrees with the sequence below n, the atoms agree and the element is the output. If they disagree at an earlier index, the two second components are disjoint, while the element lies in both, because the output along the other history lies in that history's second component.
